---
import BaseLayout from '../components/BaseLayout.astro';
import HowItWorks from '../components/HowItWorks.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How UV GO Works — Forecast Scoring & Limitations"
  description="Understand how UV GO scores daylight outings using UV, rain, feels-like temperature and wind, including rounding, missing data and forecast limitations."
  ogImage="/og/home.png"
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
    <div class="glass article-prose rounded-[22px] px-5 py-6 sm:px-8 sm:py-8">
      <h1>How UV GO chooses your time outside</h1>
      <p>
        UV GO compares forecast windows inside your free hours and chooses the one with the lowest
        average score. The rules below are shared with the planner, so the threshold tables reflect
        the implementation.
      </p>
      <h2>From forecast to a recommendation</h2>
      <p>
        Only hours marked as daytime by Open-Meteo are eligible. Starts are on the hour. A 30-minute
        outing uses one full forecast hour; longer outings use consecutive hours. For today, the
        earliest start is the current time rounded up to the next hour.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each hour receives points for UV, rain, heat, cold and wind. The window score is their
        average. Ties favour the milder worst hour, then the earlier start.
      </p>
      <h2>A worked example</h2>
      <p>
        Suppose two eligible two-hour windows have hourly scores of 2 and 4, and 3 and 3. Both
        average 3. The second window wins because its worst hour scores 3 rather than 4. These are
        illustrative scores, not a live forecast.
      </p>
      <h2>What the result cannot tell you</h2>
      <p>
        A forecast is not a live measurement. Missing factors add no points, so a low score can
        reflect incomplete data. The best available window can still be marked Not recommended. The
        score does not assess your personal medical risk.
      </p>
      <p>
        All displayed times refer to the selected place. Forecast conditions may change. Check the
        current forecast before heading out and use your own judgement.
      </p>
      <p>
        See <a href="/about/">the author and editorial approach</a>,{' '}
        <a href="/uv-index/">the UV index guide</a>, or <a href="/">plan an outing</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
    <HowItWorks />
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
